The current version uses a flat 128 KB table indexed by `num`. Message numbers are sparse, so most of that table is never read, but lookups across different numbers still land in different cache lines.

This rewrite uses a two-level page table instead:
- **Page directory:** a 256-byte array indexed by the high byte of `num`, giving a page id.
- **Shared empty page:** every high byte with no messages points to page 0, which is all `0xFFFF`.
- **Real pages:** only high bytes that have messages (typically 0x00, 0x01 and possibly 0xFF) get their own 512-byte page.

The data actually read stays within about 2 KB and stays in L1 cache. A lookup is two dependent loads.

Behavior is unchanged: when several entries share a number, the first index still wins. Unknown numbers still return `NULL`.

All lookup state is a single function-local static, built once in a lambda, so there is one guard check per call. The speedup is not measured here.

```cpp
const Profile::MESG* Profile::GetMesg(const FIT_UINT16 num)
{
    struct Lookup
    {
        FIT_UINT8 dir[256];
        FIT_UINT16 pages[257][256];
    };
    static const Lookup* const lookup = []() {
        static Lookup lk;
        for (int p = 0; p < 257; p++)
            for (int k = 0; k < 256; k++)
                lk.pages[p][k] = 0xFFFF;
        for (int h = 0; h < 256; h++)
            lk.dir[h] = 0;
        int nextPage = 1;
        for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
        {
            FIT_UINT16 n = mesgs[i].num;
            int hi = n >> 8;
            if (lk.dir[hi] == 0)
            {
                // nextPage never exceeds 256 since there are only 256 high bytes
                lk.dir[hi] = (FIT_UINT8)(nextPage <= 255 ? nextPage : 0);
                if (nextPage > 255)
                {
                    // Cannot happen in practice (only 256 possible high bytes, page 0 reserved
                    // means at most 256 pages needed => index 256 doesn't fit in uint8).
                    // Fall through handled below.
                }
                nextPage++;
            }
            FIT_UINT16* page = lk.pages[lk.dir[hi]];
            if (lk.dir[hi] != 0 && page[n & 0xFF] == 0xFFFF)
                page[n & 0xFF] = (FIT_UINT16)i;
        }
        return &lk;
    }();

    // Safety fallback for the (practically impossible) case where all 256 high bytes are used.
    FIT_UINT8 pg = lookup->dir[num >> 8];
    FIT_UINT16 idx = lookup->pages[pg][num & 0xFF];
    if (idx == 0xFFFF)
    {
        if (pg == 0 && (num >> 8) != 0)
        {
            // only reached if a page couldn't be allocated; do a linear scan
            for (int i = 0; i < MESGS; i++)
                if (mesgs[i].num == num)
                    return &mesgs[i];
        }
        return NULL;
    }
    return &mesgs[idx];
}
```